A||||20||Cooter||Handling question for 88 owners||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||01-13-2001||06:49 PM||||After completly rebuilding my front/rear suspension and lowering the car, it seems to corner very well. It is just that in the transition from straight to turn or in S turns, it feels a little "lofty" until the suspension takes a set. It is very stable after the transition and through the turn. Is this normal or am I just being too critical?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||01-13-2001||07:08 PM||||I can't say much about this... everyone always says that 88's handle a lot better than other years. Personally, I have to say that my 88 coupe handles like @$*#!<P>Of course, it is just a coupe, and I think it is partly because of the wide tires. But it doesn't hold well at all and the least amount of rain of bad weather and I won't even take the car out of the driveway!<P>I have never even driven my 87 GT so I can't compare the handling of it...<P>What all did you do to your car? I probably should do mine too. I think I want to add a steering dampener kit on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||01-13-2001||07:08 PM||||My '88 has almost no body roll, and the the transition from one direction to the opposite is almost un-noticeable. I have only rebuilt the front suspension as of now, with poly up front and KYB shocks, the rear is still original until I can put the car down for the time needed to replace. My '86 is a different story, that back-end can get very squirrely when I try, it is impossible for me to break the rear end loose on my '88.<P>I would double check front and rear sway bar endlinks, and frame mounts. After that, alignment might be the issue. I'm not a big fan of lowering springs(I'm hoping drop spindles will be available when I can afford them), so the change in suspension geometry might be the issue. Never ridden/driven with lowering springs, someone might have more insight on handling changes after adding them.<P>Tire pressure can also have a dramaitic effect on handling. I run 42-44psi in my tires, and there is a <B>huge</B> improvement over 30-35psi. I'm running Pirelli tires in stock sizing, and they wear improperly at 35psi(underinflated). Check sidewall for pressure rating, and try a little higher if tires permit(my Pirellis max at 44psi).<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-13-2001||08:20 PM||||If you mean the tail feels like it is going to brake lose than what you have is an oversteer condition I believe.<P>My car turns to wherever I point the wheel and the suspension is stock right now. I cannot even feel any body role. Mine understeers just a little. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||01-13-2001||10:25 PM||||Cooter, is it possible that the suspension is hitting the rubber bump-stops? Some of the other forum members have lowered earlier models, and it sounds like it's happened to several. I've got about 100K on my Formula, and it handles okay. Ride height, wheels and tires are stock, though. <P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||01-13-2001||10:35 PM||||It is kind of hard to explain, and it may just be my impression of the car's handling. When you first tip the car into a turn, it is almost like the car does not respond but then it quickly settles in and I have to back out of the steering a little to keep it from oversteering. It has always handled quirky, so I had hoped the new suspension would solve it. I guess that I will try another alignment and see what that does. Anybody got the spec's for the 88 so I can take it with me to the shop?||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||01-13-2001||11:10 PM||||mwbackus(remember the EGR thing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) posted '88 alignment settings a long time ago in an alignment thread(I think Oreif was the creator). You might want to try running a search for them, I know they are on here somewhere.<P>Make sure the rear cradle bolts are tight(I know the '88 doesn't use bushings), might want to check rear wheel bearings and make sure there is no more (in/out)movement than is normal, and check the rear sway bar. Did you use lowering springs in the rear too? Make sure the struts are securely attached to the towers in the frame. Those are all I can come up with right now.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||01-13-2001||11:27 PM||||Front:<P>Caster -4 1/2° to 5 1/2°, 5° optimum <BR>Camber -1/2° to 1/2°, 0° optimum<BR>Toe in 1/16° to 1/4°<P>Rear:<P>Camber -1/2° to 1 1/2°, -1° optimum<BR>Toe in 1/16° to 1/4°<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20001019-2-002912.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20001019-2-002912.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||01-14-2001||12:24 AM||||Make sure you still have ball joints first.  Then go through and replace bushings, shocks, etc. till you find what you're looking for.  If I were me, I'd keep the same springs and used drop spindles If you feel a need to lower your car.  There's just no good way to drop the car when you change the geometry of the front end.  With all the engineering work that went into the 88's suspension, don't you think they would have decreased that fender/ tire gap in front if it was at all possible (financial concerns realized).  Shocks are your best bet for a better handling car.  Konis are expensive, but they are available and they're proven.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||01-14-2001||12:34 AM||||And tires.  Tires matter.  If you really feel a need to outcorner anything on the road, get yourself some Yoko AVS intermediates or something else with a treadwear rating under 200.  They make a huge difference.<P>Alignment settings:<P>As much freaking camber as you struts will allow in the rear.  I run -2 degrees all the time, and when I race, I put more in like -3 degrees.<P>Your front will stick better, making for a more neutral handling car, if you lower the tire pressure to around 23 psi in front, and use soft shock settings.  Also run with a toe out on the front if you're racing.  If you're not, keep it zeroed out.<P>This is serious real world stuff.  Making the SOB handle well doesn't mean making it look pretty.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||01-14-2001||12:52 AM||||Eric,<P>As far as I know, there are no drop spindles available for the '88. If you know of any available, I would love to know the source. It is also my understanding from Cooter's post, that the entire suspension is new, bushings, etc...<P>How does lowering tire pressure improve handling? It is my understanding that you will be running on the sidewalls on cornering, reducing your traction on the surface. I always notice a tremendous improvement on cornering ability, when I inflate my tires much higher than 23psi. Seems to me, that the lower tire pressure wold create terrible sidewall flex, making for slower steering response, reduced cornering abilities, and a generally un-fun car to drive. I'm not a suspension/handling guru, so I'm curious if I'm missing something here.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||RaeQwan||01-14-2001||01:02 AM||||my 88 likes to dance, its rear wiggles alot.. just driving straight, I do believe my struts are dead  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I'm replacing em when I fixed the rear clip in a few weeks<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwansig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cheever2||01-14-2001||02:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>When you first tip the car into a turn, it is almost like the car does not respond but then it quickly settles in and I have to back out of the steering a little </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>I get a little of that, happens in a fraction of a second, but noticeable.  Always thought it was normal for these cars.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula||01-14-2001||09:20 AM||||I have new struts and shocks and I have no body roll when doing an s-turn||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||01-14-2001||10:55 AM||||Thanks for all the replies- I really appreciate it! I went out in the car again last night a took a blast around Mt. Cheaha just see if I could get a better feel for what is happening. <BR>The car is stuck like glue, but the first instant of a change in direction it feels like the body shifts over the tires and then takes a set. It is not so bad that I can't live with it, I just don't know if it is normal or not. I will take the above spec's with me to have the shop check the alighnment again and see what that shows.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Eric||01-14-2001||01:47 PM||||I don't think alignment is your problem.  The transition problems are probably a result of bad struts and a sloppy rear suspension.  I don't think Pontiac put stiff enough springs in the rear because they were worried about the oversteer issue.  You also have to get it realigned after you mess around with it.  Tell us how much of your suspension you replaced.  Coil overs may be the way to go.<P>I really only notice slop in the rear end when I steer the car back and forth at high speed.  I honestly think that's just sidewall flex too.<P>56, lowering the tire pressure in front gives the tire a bigger contact patch.  Lets the front end stick more kind of like taking off the sway bar.  This holds true for autocrossing only where the runs are less than one minute and you don't have to worry about heating up the tires too much.  On the street I run about 32 psi in front and 35 in the rear.  The drop spindles for the 88s used to be available through Ryanne, but I guess they closed shop.  I think they were like $500 a pair or something like that.  Way too expensive to even look twice at.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||01-14-2001||02:52 PM||||Cooter, is this a coupe we're talking about? My '86SE has terrible oversteer when pushed through a corner hard, or if I even slightly start swinging the car left and right. If I ever decide to do something with it, other than turn it into my daily driver, first thing will be a rear sway bar, and possibly a stiffer set of springs, the stock ones in it, are too soft.<P>My '88 has no body roll, and is impossible to get the rear end to even hint at breaking loose, at higher speeds it is possible to make the rear end feel a little lively, but as Eric said, I think it's sidewall flex. Do you have rear sway bar on your '88 Cooter?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fieroboy1||01-14-2001||07:35 PM||||Bell tech makes dropped spindles for practically anything. My friend has a pair on his 86 se.  If they don't already make a set for 88's they can make a dropped spindle for anything on the road.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Cooter||01-14-2001||10:13 PM||||So far, I have replaced all suspension rubber with polyurethane, new shocks, struts, ball joints, inner/outer rod ends and rack bushing. The wheels are 16 in. Sunfire GT's with 205/55 tires that have less than 500 miles on them. I am going to check the rear sway bar links and mounts to see if anything is loose or broken. Hopefully there is some little thing that I have missed and it will be easy to correct.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Eric||01-15-2001||12:06 AM||||Well Cooter, unless you used spacers, the wheels may be your problem.  I have to think the Sunfire wheels have too much offset and narrow your rear track.  Plus the tires are the same size as the front which is not a good thing especially when downsizing to a narrower tire.  If you have a set of original 88 wheels with good tires, slap them on your car and see how it feels.  ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cooter||01-15-2001||01:00 AM||||It was the same before going to the Sunfire wheels. Good call though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
